      "text": "To find the function \\( f(x) \\), we start with the given equation:\n\\[ f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2. \\]\nWe will substitute \\( x = 3 \\) into the equation to find \\( f(3) \\), but first, we will need to find \\( f(2) \\) and \\( f(0) \\) as well.\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x = 2 \\) into the equation:\n\\[ f(2) + 2f(-1) = 3 \\cdot 2^2 = 12. \\]\nThis gives us the first equation:\n\\[ f(2) + 2f(-1) = 12. \\tag{1} \\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( x = 0 \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ f(0) + 2f(1) = 3 \\cdot 0^2 = 0. \\]\nThis gives us the second equation:\n\\[ f(0) + 2f(1) = 0. \\tag{2} \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( x = 1 \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ f(1) + 2f(0) = 3 \\cdot 1^2 = 3. \\]\nThis gives us the third equation:\n\\[ f(1) + 2f(0) = 3. \\tag{3} \\]\n\nWe now have a system of three equations:\n1. \\( f(2) + 2f(-1) = 12 \\)\n2. \\( f(0) + 2f(1) = 0 \\)\n3. \\( f(1) + 2f(0) = 3 \\)\n\nWe will solve this system step by step. From equation (2), solve for \\( f(0) \\):\n\\[ f(0) = -2f(1). \\tag{4} \\]\n\nSubstitute \\( f(0) = -2f(1) \\) into equation (3):\n\\[ f(1) + 2(-2f(1)) = 3, \\]\n\\[ f(1) - 4f(1) = 3, \\]\n\\[ -3f(1) = 3, \\]\n\\[ f(1) = -1. \\tag{5} \\]\n\nNow substitute \\( f(1) = -1 \\) into equation (4):\n\\[ f(0) = -2(-1) = 2. \\tag{6} \\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( f(1) = -1 \\) and \\( f(0) = 2 \\) into equation (1):\n\\[ f(2) + 2f(-1) = 12. \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( f(-1) \\). Substitute \\( f(1) = -1 \\) and \\( f(0) = 2 \\) into the original equation with \\( x = -1 \\):\n\\[ f(-1) + 2f(2) = 3 \\cdot (-1)^2 = 3. \\]\nThis gives us the fourth equation:\n\\[ f(-1) + 2f(2) = 3. \\tag{7} \\]\n\nWe now have two equations involving \\( f(2) \\) and \\( f(-1) \\):\n1. \\( f(2) + 2f(-1) = 12 \\)\n2. \\( f(-1) + 2f(2) = 3 \\)\n\nWe can solve these equations simultaneously. First, multiply equation (7) by 2:\n\\[ 2f(-1) + 4f(2) = 6. \\tag{8} \\]\n\nNow subtract equation (8) from equation (1):\n\\[ (f(2) + 2f(-1)) - (2f(-1) + 4f(2)) = 12 - 6, \\]\n\\[ f(2) + 2f(-1) - 2f(-1) - 4f(2) = 6, \\]\n\\[ -3f(2) = 6, \\]\n\\[ f(2) = -2. \\]\n\nNow substitute \\( f(2) = -2 \\) back into equation (7):\n\\[ f(-1) + 2(-2) = 3, \\]\n\\[ f(-1) - 4 = 3, \\]\n\\[ f(-1) = 7. \\]\n\nNow we have \\( f(2) = -2 \\) and \\( f(-1) = 7 \\). To find \\( f(3) \\), substitute \\( x = 3 \\) into the original equation:\n\\[ f(3) + 2f(-2) = 3 \\cdot 3^2 = 27. \\]\nWe need to find \\( f(-2) \\). Substitute \\( x = -2 \\):\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(3) = 3 \\cdot (-2)^2 = 12. \\]\nThis gives us the fifth equation:\n\\[ f(-2) + 2f(3) = 12. \\tag{9} \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( f(3) \\). Substitute \\( f(2) = -2 \\) and \\( f(-1) = 7 \\) into the original equation with \\( x = 1 \\):\n\\[ f(1) + 2f(-1) = 3 \\cdot 1^2 = 3. \\]\nThis is consistent with \\( f(1) = -1 \\), so we use the values we already have. Substitute \\( f(3) = y \\) into the equation for \\( f(2) \\):\n\\[ -2 + 2y = 27, \\]\n\\[ 2y = 29, \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{29}{2}. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( f(3) \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{29}{2}}\\).",
