      "text": "To find \\( f(3) \\), we need to use the given functional equation and determine the function \\( f(x) \\) first. The functional equation states:\n\n\\[\nf(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2 \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe will find a second equation by substituting \\( x = 1 - x \\) into the original equation. This gives:\n\n\\[\nf(1 - x) + 2f(x) = 3(1 - x)^2\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[\nf(1 - x) + 2f(x) = 3(1 - 2x + x^2) = 3 - 6x + 3x^2\n\\]\n\nNow we have two equations:\n1. \\( f(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2 \\)\n2. \\( f(1 - x) + 2f(x) = 3 - 6x + 3x^2 \\)\n\nTo eliminate \\( f(1 - x) \\), we can multiply the first equation by 2 and then subtract the second equation from it:\n\n\\[\n2f(x) + 4f(1 - x) = 6x^2 \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf(1 - x) + 2f(x) = 3 - 6x + 3x^2 \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSubtract equation (3) from equation (2):\n\n\\[\n(2f(x) + 4f(1 - x)) - (f(1 - x) + 2f(x)) = 6x^2 - (3 - 6x + 3x^2)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n3f(1 - x) = 6x^2 - 3 + 6x - 3x^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n3f(1 - x) = 3x^2 + 6x - 3\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf(1 - x) = x^2 + 2x - 1\n\\]\n\nNow substitute \\( f(1 - x) = x^2 + 2x - 1 \\) back into the original equation:\n\n\\[\nf(x) + 2(x^2 + 2x - 1) = 3x^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf(x) + 2x^2 + 4x - 2 = 3x^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf(x) = 3x^2 - 2x^2 - 4x + 2\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf(x) = x^2 - 4x + 2\n\\]\n\nNow we can find \\( f(3) \\):\n\n\\[\nf(3) = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 + 2 = 9 - 12 + 2 = -1\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}\n\\]",
